    <!-- Does Moscow Use DST? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>Does Moscow Use Daylight Saving Time?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No. Moscow currently does not observe daylight saving time.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Russia has a unique history with daylight saving policies. In 2011, Russia adopted permanent year-round daylight saving time (placing Moscow at UTC+4). However, due to public discontent with dark winter mornings, the Russian parliament passed legislation in July 2014 that permanently shifted Moscow Standard Time to <strong>UTC+3</strong> starting October 26, 2014.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since October 2014, MSK has remained permanently fixed at UTC+3 year-round without seasonal adjustments. This stability means the GMT to MSK conversion is always <strong>GMT + 3 hours</strong> regardless of the time of year.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Date Changes -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>GMT to MSK Date Changes</h2>
      <p>
        Because MSK is 3 hours ahead of GMT, late-evening GMT times pass midnight and fall on the following calendar day in MSK. This is an essential detail when managing deadlines, flight departures, and international communication.
      </p>
      <h3>Date Rollover Window</h3>
      <ul style="padding-left: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.9;">
        <li><strong>GMT 00:00 to 20:59</strong> → MSK 03:00 to 23:59 → <strong>Same calendar day</strong></li>
        <li><strong>GMT 21:00</strong> → MSK 00:00 (midnight) → <strong>Next calendar day</strong></li>
        <li><strong>GMT 22:00</strong> → MSK 01:00 AM → <strong>Next calendar day</strong></li>
        <li><strong>GMT 23:00</strong> → MSK 02:00 AM → <strong>Next calendar day</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Examples of Date Transition</h3>
      <div style="background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem;"><strong>Example 1:</strong> 8:00 PM GMT on April 15 → 11:00 PM MSK on April 15 (same day)</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem;"><strong>Example 2:</strong> 10:00 PM GMT on April 15 → 1:00 AM MSK on April 16 (date advances)</p>
        <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Example 3:</strong> 11:00 PM GMT on December 31 → 2:00 AM MSK on January 1 (new year in MSK!)</p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Always check both the <strong>time and date</strong> when converting late GMT hours. Our converter tool handles date transitions automatically.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Meetings and Business -->
